<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tomasz Dąbal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Tomasz Dabal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tomasz Dąbal (1890–1937) was a Polish lawyer and peasant activist who in November 1918 led, with the priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugeniusz-okon">Eugeniusz Okoń</a>, the short-lived Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarnobrzeg">Tarnobrzeg</a>, which demanded land reform. He later became a communist deputy, moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and was executed there during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a> in 1937.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tomasz-dabal.jpg" alt="Tomasz Dąbal" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomasz_D%C4%85bal_(-1922).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tomasz Jan Dąbal was born on 29 December 1890 in Sobów. From 1909 to 1914 he studied law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and medicine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, and in 1911 he joined the Polish People's Party. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> he served in the 87th Infantry Regiment of the Austro-Hungarian Army on the Russian, Balkan and Italian fronts, was wounded three times and reached the rank of captain. In January 1917 he became a machine-gun instructor in the 3rd Legion Infantry Regiment, and near the end of the war he was held for two months in a camp in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udine">Udine</a> for political activity in the army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In early November 1918, as a special representative of the Polish Liquidation Commission in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, he took command of the gendarmerie in the counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarnobrzeg">Tarnobrzeg</a>, Mielec, Nisko and Kolbuszowa and turned it into a People's Militia. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugeniusz-okon">Eugeniusz Okoń</a> he founded the Republic of Tarnobrzeg on 6 November 1918. He belonged to the Polish People's Party &quot;Left&quot;, then to the Radical Peasant Party, which he founded with Okoń, and sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> from 1918 to 1921.</p><p>Dąbal joined the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1920. A few days before the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> he told the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a>: &quot;I do not consider the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> an enemy. On the contrary, I welcome it as a friend of the Polish Nation.&quot; In July 1921 he and Stanisław Łańcucki formed the Sejm Faction of Communist Deputies. Stripped of his immunity in November 1921 and tried for attempting to overthrow the state, he was sentenced on 7 July 1922 to six years of hard labour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1923 he left for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in an exchange of political prisoners. He worked in the Central Committee of the Communist Party of Byelorussia, was deputy secretary general of the Peasant International from 1923 to 1928 and held posts in the International Red Aid and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a>. He studied at the Institute of Red Professors from 1930 to 1932, took a doctorate in economics in 1934 and became vice-president of the Belarusian Academy of Sciences in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a>.</p><p>Like most Polish communists in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, he was arrested and executed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a>, after a confession was extracted from him that he had directed the Polish Military Organization throughout the Soviet Union. The Military Collegium of the Supreme Court of the USSR rehabilitated him on 21 December 1955.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tomasz Dąbal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In November 1918, as Austrian authority in Galicia collapsed, a radical priest, Eugeniusz Okon, and the peasant activist <b>Tomasz Dabal</b> called on peasants and returning soldiers around Tarnobrzeg to take power.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-tarnobrzeg">Republic of Tarnobrzeg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sobów, Tarnobrzeg, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in Lefortovo Prison, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.6,21.74&amp;year=1920" aria-label="Sobów, Tarnobrzeg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Tomasz Dąbal"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4553 5772h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4553 5772h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.6,21.74&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
